      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by using the fact that \\(\\alpha\\) is inversely proportional to \\(\\beta\\). This relationship can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[\n\\alpha \\beta = k\n\\]\nwhere \\(k\\) is a constant.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the constant \\(k\\)\nWe are given that \\(\\alpha = 4\\) when \\(\\beta = 9\\). Substituting these values into the equation \\(\\alpha \\beta = k\\), we get:\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 9 = k\n\\]\n\\[\nk = 36\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Use the constant \\(k\\) to find \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\)\nNow that we have the constant \\(k = 36\\), we can use it to find \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\). Substituting \\(\\beta = -72\\) into the equation \\(\\alpha \\beta = k\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\alpha \\cdot (-72) = 36\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Solve for \\(\\alpha\\)\nTo isolate \\(\\alpha\\), we divide both sides of the equation by \\(-72\\):\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\frac{36}{-72}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\) is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\).",
